# ============================================================================
# Mawzun Backend — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` (`cp .env.example .env`) — every value below is the real,
# working local-development configuration. Only the Mailtrap token is a
# placeholder because it is an account-bound secret.
#
# How the backend reads these (src/config/env.ts — zod-validated, fails fast
# at boot with a readable message):
#   - Unknown vars in this file are ignored (e.g. APP_DB_PASSWORD and
#     MIGRATION__APPLYKEY are no longer read by any code and are kept only
#     for reference).
#   - MAILTRAP__* has a legacy double-underscore spelling; mail.ts accepts
#     both MAILTRAP__APITOKEN and MAILTRAP_API_TOKEN (same for the other two).
# ============================================================================

# --- Server -----------------------------------------------------------------
PORT=5000
NODE_ENV=development

# --- Database (PostgreSQL) --------------------------------------------------
# Two connections, deliberately:
#   DATABASE_URL        - the application role ("mawzun_app"). It does not
#                         BYPASSRLS, so every request-time query is filtered by
#                         the row-level security policy.
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL - the privileged role (local dev: "postgres"). Used by
#                         migrations, seeding, and the few paths that must
#                         resolve identity before a company is known (login,
#                         signup, refresh), plus platform administration.
#
# DEPLOYING WITH ONE SHARED ROLE (e.g. Heroku Postgres exposes a single URL):
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL may be OMITTED entirely — it defaults to DATABASE_URL.
# provision-db.mjs detects the shared role and skips provisioning. IMPORTANT:
# the table owner bypasses RLS, so database-level tenant isolation is
# disabled — isolation then relies solely on the application-level companyId
# scoping that wraps every query (scoped() in src/shared/request.ts, verified
# by tests). Prefer two roles when the host allows it so the database itself
# enforces isolation as defense in depth.
#
# CREDENTIAL ROTATION (Heroku rotates credentials periodically): the running
# process resolves its connection strings at boot. After a rotation, restart
# the dyno (`heroku restart` — or use `heroku pg:credentials:rotate` which
# requires it) so the new URL is picked up. Only DATABASE_URL needs to exist;
# nothing else has to be kept in sync.
#
# The app role is provisioned automatically on every boot
# (scripts/provision-db.mjs via `npm run db:provision` and the start script):
# it creates/updates the role from DATABASE_URL and re-grants schema access,
# so a migrate reset or dropped role self-heals on the next start.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://mawzun_app:CHANGE_ME_app_password@localhost:5432/mawzun?schema=public"
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:CHANGE_ME_owner_password@localhost:5432/mawzun?schema=public"

# Legacy/unused: kept because old provisioning docs reference it. The role
# password now lives inside DATABASE_URL only.
APP_DB_PASSWORD="CHANGE_ME_app_password"

# --- JWT Authentication (src/config/env.ts, src/utils/jwt.ts) ---------------
# Minimum 16 chars (zod). The value below is the real LOCAL DEV key — replace
# with a 64+ char random string in production.
JWT_KEY="CHANGE_ME_generate_a_random_string_at_least_16_chars"
JWT_ISSUER="Mawzun"
JWT_AUDIENCE="Mawzun-Client"
JWT_ACCESS_MINUTES=60
JWT_REFRESH_MINUTES=1440
# "Remember me" refresh lifetime (30 days)
JWT_REFRESH_REMEMBER_MINUTES=43200

# Legacy spellings from the previous config system — NOT read by current code.
#JWT__DURATIONINMINUTESFORACCESSTOKEN=60
#JWT__DURATIONINMINUTESFORREFRESHTOKEN=1440
#JWT__DURATIONINMINUTESFORREFRESHTOKENWITHREMEMBERME=43200

# --- Mailtrap (email) — src/utils/mail.ts -----------------------------------
# The only genuinely secret value here: create a free account at
# https://mailtrap.io and paste your inbox API token. Without it the backend
# boots fine and only logs a warning when something tries to send email.
MAILTRAP__APITOKEN="your-mailtrap-api-token-here"
MAILTRAP__SENDEREMAIL="hello@demomailtrap.co"
MAILTRAP__SENDERNAME="Mawzun"
# Modern spellings also accepted (same values, pick one style):
# MAILTRAP_API_TOKEN / MAILTRAP_SENDER_EMAIL / MAILTRAP_SENDER_NAME

# --- CORS (src/app.ts) ------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated allowlist. In non-production, any http://localhost:<port>
# origin is additionally allowed automatically (Vite picks a free port), so
# the 8081 entry is for clarity. Use "*" to allow everything (dev only).
CORS_ORIGIN="http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:8081,https://mawzun.org,https://app.mawzun.org,https://mawzun-homepage.workers.dev,https://mawzun-app.workers.dev"

# --- Seeding (prisma/seed.ts) -----------------------------------------------
# true  = fill the demo company شركة النور للتجارة with full Arabic sample
#         data (owner@mawzun.local / Owner@123, staff1..4@mawzun.local /
#         Staff@123, 120 orders, catalog, stock). Idempotent: reruns skip a
#         company that already has orders.
# false = only ensure platform-level reference rows + countries (what the UI reads).
SEED_DEMO_DATA=true

# --- Legacy/unused -----------------------------------------------------------
# MIGRATION__APPLYKEY — the programmatic migration-apply key from the previous
# architecture. No code reads it today (migrations run through
# `prisma migrate deploy` on boot). Kept for reference.
MIGRATION__APPLYKEY="your-secure-migration-apply-key-here"

# --- Cloudflare R2 object storage (product image uploads) -------------------
# src/shared/storage.ts. All five values are required for uploads to work;
# without them the API answers with a clear "not configured" error instead of
# storing dead links. Create an R2 bucket + API token in the Cloudflare
# dashboard, then enable the bucket's public access (custom domain or r2.dev).
R2_ACCOUNT_ID="your-cloudflare-account-id"
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID="your-r2-access-key-id"
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="your-r2-secret-access-key"
R2_BUCKET="mawzun-images"
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL="https://images.example.com"
#   R2_JURISDICTION     - OPTIONAL: set to "eu" or "fedramp" ONLY if the bucket
#                         shows that jurisdiction badge in the dashboard.
#                         Jurisdiction buckets are invisible from the default
#                         endpoint (NoSuchBucket despite valid credentials).
R2_JURISDICTION="eu"


# --- Redis rate limiting (Layerbase Cloud) — src/lib/rateLimit.ts ------------
# Unset REDIS_URL = every limiter fails open (requests pass, error logged).
# Each limiter has its own kill switch: 0/false/no disables it, unset = on.
#
# Layerbase serves every database on ONE shared port and routes by the TLS SNI
# hostname, so the URL MUST use the rediss:// scheme. The client sets the TLS
# servername from the URL host automatically (see tlsOptionsFor in rateLimit.ts)
# — without it the handshake is unroutable and the connection is dropped.
# REDIS_URL=rediss://default:<password>@<host>.cloud.layerbase.dev:6379
REDIS_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_DISABLED=0
# RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DISABLED=0
# RATE_LIMIT_SESSION_DISABLED=0
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_WINDOW_MS=900000
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX=30
RATE_LIMIT_RESET_WINDOW_MS=86400000
RATE_LIMIT_RESET_MAX=1
RATE_LIMIT_SESSION_WINDOW_MS=60000
RATE_LIMIT_SESSION_MAX=300

# Seals Shopify access tokens at rest (AES-256-GCM); min 16 chars
APP_SECRET=

# Public URL of the shopify_integration worker. When set, saving a SHOPIFY store
# auto-registers the sync webhooks on that store via the Admin API.
# SHOPIFY_WEBHOOK_BASE_URL=https://<your-worker>.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev

# SaaS-wide Shopify kill switch. UNSET/0 = Shopify disabled (SaaS default).
# Self-hosted instances set SHOPIFY_FEATURE_ENABLED=1 to enable the sync.
# SHOPIFY_FEATURE_ENABLED=1

# --- Grafana Cloud observability (optional; unset = push disabled) ---------
# Remote write target + credentials. All three must be set for metrics to
# reach Grafana Cloud; the /api/v1/metrics endpoint serves regardless.
GRAFANA_METRICS_PUSH_URL=https://prometheus-prod-XX-prod-region-1.grafana.net/api/prom/push
GRAFANA_METRICS_INSTANCE_ID=YOUR_GRAFANA_INSTANCE_ID
# Paste your Grafana Cloud API token here (never commit the real value).
GRAFANA_API_TOKEN=
# Email contact used by scripts/provision-grafana-alerts.mjs when creating
# alert rules (informational to the app itself).
# GRAFANA_ALERT_EMAIL=ops@example.com

# --- Observability kill switches (all default to ENABLED) -------------------
# Value semantics: "1"/"true"/"yes"/"on" (any case) disables the named piece.
# Unset or ANY OTHER value = enabled — the parser fails open, so a typo can
# never silently blind observability. Restart the dyno after changing.
#
# METRICS_DISABLED=1
#     Master switch: no metric collection at all. /api/v1/metrics serves an
#     empty registry, remote write never starts, business-gauge COUNT queries
#     are skipped. Use to opt an environment out of Grafana entirely.
# METRICS_HTTP_DISABLED=1
#     Disables the HTTP family: mawzun_http_requests_total,
#     mawzun_http_request_duration_seconds, mawzun_http_errors_total.
#     (Also stops the error-breadcrumb path from bumping http_errors_total.)
# METRICS_AUTH_DISABLED=1
#     Disables mawzun_auth_events_total.
# METRICS_BUSINESS_DISABLED=1
#     Disables mawzun_companies_active / mawzun_users_active /
#     mawzun_sync_errors_pending AND the COUNT(*) queries behind them —
#     useful if you want zero metrics-driven DB load.
# METRICS_PUSH_DISABLED=1
#     Collect + serve /api/v1/metrics but never remote-write to Grafana.
# METRICS_PUSH_INTERVAL_MS=15000
#     Push cadence. Minimum 5000 (enforced at boot; lower values clamp).
# LOG_BREADCRUMBS_DISABLED=1
#     captureError() becomes a no-op: no error_log writes anywhere.
METRICS_PUSH_INTERVAL_MS=15000

# Dedicated logs database (optional but recommended for production).
# Postgres holding ONLY the error_log breadcrumb table (schema-on-write: the
# table is created automatically on the first breadcrumb). When unset,
# breadcrumbs fall back to the main application database.
# Free options: Neon / Supabase / Aiven free tier.
#
# Each breadcrumb records the failure (route, method, status, requestId), the
# client (ipAddress, userAgent), the tenant (userId, companyId as ids only) and
# the REDACTED request body — which is what turns "validation failed" into
# "validation failed on {quantity: -5}". Passwords, tokens, cookies and card
# data are stripped before the write, and /auth/* bodies are withheld whole
# because those bodies ARE the credentials. The rules live in
# src/observability/redact.ts; verify them with `npx tsx verify-redaction.mjs`.
#
# The trail is personal data (IP + User-Agent). It is pruned to the newest 200
# rows on every write, read only by platform staff in the admin panel, and never
# returned by the API.
LOG_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Connection budgets ------------------------------------------------------
#
# POSTGRES. Heroku Postgres essential-0 allows 20 connections total; this dyno's
# share is 10, so the budget is deliberately one number rather than a sum you
# have to compute. The backend splits it 70/30 across its two clients:
#
#   app   (7) — carries all request concurrency, so it gets the larger share
#   admin (3) — pre-auth lookups, signup, token rotation, platform admin
#
# The other services each take 2, so the whole fleet stays under 20:
#   Backend 10 + ai-api 2 + shopify-webhook 2 + admin 2 = 16, leaving 4 spare.
#
# The worker figure is a per-REQUEST client (workerd forbids sharing I/O across
# requests), so `max: 2` is how many queries one request may run at once — not a
# process-wide pool. Behind Hyperdrive they are multiplexed upstream and do not
# consume Postgres connections at all.
#
# Scale guidance: web=1 dyno keep 10; web=2 dynos set DB_POOL_MAX=5 (2x5=10).
DB_POOL_MAX=10

# REDIS. The backend holds a real connection pool, sized here.
#
# Connections are created ONCE on first rate-limited request and reused for the
# life of the process — nothing opens a connection per request. A process that
# never checks a limit holds zero sockets.
#
# Budget across the fleet: Backend 10, every other service 2.
#
# What the pool does and does not buy — worth reading before raising it:
# Redis executes commands on a SINGLE thread, so N connections do NOT multiply
# throughput the way N Postgres connections do. ioredis already pipelines on one
# socket, so one connection handles concurrent checks fine. The pool bounds
# concurrency and limits head-of-line blocking; it is not a parallelism
# multiplier. 2 is a good default — raise it only if you measure contention.
REDIS_POOL_MAX=2

# --- Platform admin minting (scripts/mint-platform-admin.mjs) ---------------
# Email for the platform admin the script mints (default admin@mawzun.org).
# PLATFORM_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@mawzun.org


# --- Pagination ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The largest page a list endpoint will serve. A CEILING, not a default: each
# endpoint keeps its own default (25, or 100 for roles) and a larger request is
# rejected by validation rather than silently truncated.
#
# Lower it to bound how much work one request may ask for; raise it on a
# self-hosted instance with a fast database and few rows. Raising it is not
# free — the value multiplies the rows a single query returns, so a large
# ceiling on a big table turns one cheap request into an expensive one.
PAGINATION_MAX_PAGE_SIZE=200

# Largest image a client may upload, in BYTES (default 10 MiB = 10485760).
# Enforced in three places, all reading this one value: the multer limit on both
# upload routes (/uploads and product images), and a raw-bytes check in
# shared/storage.ts so a caller that bypasses HTTP still cannot store an
# oversized object. The rejection message is generated from this number, so the
# Arabic text always names the limit actually in force.
#
# Raising it raises the worst-case memory per concurrent upload: multer buffers
# the whole file in RAM before it reaches storage, so a large value on a small
# dyno is a memory-exhaustion risk.
UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=10485760
